{{Short description|Overview of agriculture, forestry, and fishing in the Baliscan Confederation}}
{{Economy of Balisca}}

'''Agriculture, forestry, and fishing in Balisca''' form the {{W|primary sector of industry}} of the [[Economy of Balisca|Baliscan economy]] together with the [[Mining in Balisca|mining industry]]. The sector accounts for approximately 4.2% of gross domestic product and employs about 5.8% of the workforce (11.9 million people) as of 2023. While the primary sector's share of GDP has declined significantly since the [[Baliscan Revolution]], it remains strategically important for food security, rural livelihoods, and environmental stewardship.

Balisca's agricultural sector is characterized by its cooperative structure, ecological orientation, and integration with the confederation's broader social and economic systems. Unlike market-based agricultural systems, Baliscan agriculture operates under principles of [[W:Decommodification|decommodification]], with production organized primarily through [[agricultural cooperative]]s and [[communal land tenure]] systems. This model emerged from the revolutionary land reforms of the 1920s and has been refined through subsequent policy initiatives including the [[Low Impact Initiative]] and the [[Ecological Action]] program.

The confederation's diverse geography—ranging from the temperate [[Marañón rainforest]] to the highland [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] mountain range to the fertile coastal plains—supports a wide variety of agricultural activities. Major products include [[W:Wheat|wheat]], [[W:Barley|barley]], [[W:Corn|corn]], [[W:Soybean|soybeans]], [[W:Wine|wine]] grapes, [[W:Fruit|tree fruits]], [[W:Vegetable|vegetables]], [[W:Cattle|beef and dairy]], [[W:Timber|timber]], and [[W:Seafood|seafood]]. Balisca is largely self-sufficient in staple foods and is a significant exporter of wine, fruit, timber, fish products, and soybeans.

==History==
{{Main|History of agriculture in Balisca}}

===Pre-revolutionary period (before 1920)===

Prior to the [[Baliscan Revolution]], agriculture in the territory that now comprises Balisca was characterized by extreme inequality and inefficient land use. Under colonial and post-colonial administrations, agricultural land was concentrated in the hands of a small elite—primarily large landowners ([[W:Hacienda|hacendados]]), foreign investors, and commercial interests. The majority of the rural population worked as [[W:Sharecropping|sharecroppers]], [[W:Debt peonage|debt peons]], or [[W:Landless worker|landless laborers]], with little control over their livelihoods.

The agricultural system was oriented toward export commodities—particularly [[sugar]], [[coffee]], [[cotton]], and [[beef]]—rather than domestic food production. This export orientation left the population vulnerable to international price fluctuations and food shortages. Rural poverty was widespread, and agricultural productivity was low by international standards.

===Revolutionary land reform (1920–1935)===

The [[Baliscan Revolution]] (1919–1920) fundamentally transformed agricultural land tenure. Revolutionary committees, often organized by [[W:Peasant|peasant]] unions and indigenous communities, seized large estates and redistributed land to those who worked it. The [[Basic Law: Land and Agriculture]] (1921) established the legal framework for this transformation:

* The abolition of large private landholdings ([[W:Latifundium|latifundia]])
* The transfer of agricultural land to cooperative and communal ownership
* The establishment of [[communal land trust]]s to hold land in perpetuity
* The guarantee of land access for all who wished to farm
* The prohibition of land speculation and absentee ownership

By 1935, approximately 85% of agricultural land had been transferred to cooperative or communal ownership. The remaining 15% consisted of small family farms that chose to maintain individual tenure, subject to limits on land area and prohibitions on speculative sale.

===Consolidation and modernization (1935–1970)===

The decades following the revolution saw significant investment in agricultural modernization. The [[Confederal Agricultural Institute]] (1923) provided research, extension services, and training to cooperative farmers. The [[Agricultural Mechanization Program]] (1948–1965) expanded access to tractors, harvesters, and other equipment through cooperative ownership pools.

Agricultural productivity increased substantially during this period. The introduction of improved crop varieties, irrigation systems, and soil conservation techniques enabled Balisca to achieve food self-sufficiency by the early 1960s. The cooperative model proved effective at spreading the benefits of mechanization and technical innovation across the rural population.

However, this period also saw significant rural-to-urban migration, as mechanization reduced labor requirements and urban industrial jobs offered higher incomes. The agricultural workforce declined from 42% of total employment in 1940 to 18% in 1970, though the absolute number of agricultural workers remained substantial given Balisca's growing population.

===The Special Period and agricultural transformation (1977–1990)===

{{Main|Special Period|Low Impact Initiative}}

The [[Special Period]] of the late 1970s, precipitated by the [[Camboriú War]] and [[Bight of Ayorin Crisis]], created acute fuel and input shortages that disrupted conventional agricultural practices. Rather than viewing this as a temporary crisis, the Baliscan government used it as an opportunity to fundamentally reorient agriculture toward sustainability and self-sufficiency.

The [[Low Impact Initiative]] (1977/1983) catalyzed a comprehensive transformation of agricultural practices. With synthetic fertilizers and pesticides in short supply, cooperatives transitioned to [[W:Organic farming|organic farming]] ([[W:Organopónicos|organopónicos]]) methods. This transition proved so successful that organic practices became standard. The initiative also promoted local food systems, with the [[W:15-minute city|15-minute city]] concept extended to food systems through emphasis on local production and distribution. Urban agriculture expanded massively through community gardens, rooftop farms, and peri-urban cooperatives. Seed sovereignty was strengthened through development of locally adapted seed varieties via cooperative breeding programs. Energy independence was achieved through solar-powered irrigation, electric farm vehicles, and biogas production, reducing dependence on fossil fuels.

By 1990, Balisca had achieved one of the highest rates of organic agricultural production in the world, with over 70% of agricultural land under organic management. The crisis-driven innovations of the Special Period became permanent features of Baliscan agriculture.

===Contemporary period (1990–present)===

The post-Special Period era has seen continued refinement of Baliscan agricultural practices. The program proposed [[Ecological Action]] in 1995, further integrated agricultural policy with environmental protection, establishing strict standards for soil conservation, water quality, and biodiversity protection. The [[Food Sovereignty Initiative]] (2008) strengthened local food systems and reduced dependence on imported inputs.

Today, Baliscan agriculture is characterized by high productivity achieved through ecological methods rather than chemical intensification, strong cooperative organization at all levels of the food system, integration of agriculture with forestry, fishing, and environmental stewardship, emphasis on food quality, nutritional value, and cultural appropriateness, and resilience to climate variability and external shocks.

==Land use and geography==

===Agricultural land===

Balisca's total land area of approximately 2,847,000 square kilometres (1,099,000 sq mi) includes diverse agricultural zones. Approximately 187,000 km² (6.6% of total land area) is classified as arable land under permanent cultivation, while an additional 412,000 km² (14.5%) is used for permanent pasture and grazing. Forest land covers approximately 1,124,000 km² (39.5%), much of it under sustainable forestry management.

The relatively small proportion of arable land reflects Balisca's distinctive geography: the rugged [[Matarrañya]] highlands, the dense [[Marañón rainforest]], and the arid southern regions limit agricultural expansion. However, the available agricultural land is intensively and efficiently cultivated, supporting a population of over 200 million.

