==Cooperative organization==

===Agricultural cooperatives===

The cooperative model is the dominant organizational form in Baliscan agriculture. As of 2023, there were approximately 52,400 agricultural cooperatives with 14.2 million member households. These cooperatives range from small specialized producer groups to large multi-purpose organizations integrating production, processing, and marketing.

Types of agricultural cooperatives include production cooperatives where members farm collectively on cooperative land, marketing cooperatives where independent farmers jointly market their products, supply cooperatives handling joint purchasing of inputs and equipment, processing cooperatives with cooperative ownership of processing facilities, and multi-purpose cooperatives integrating multiple functions.

The [[Confederal Cooperative Federation]] coordinates activities among agricultural cooperatives and represents their interests in policy discussions.

===Community-supported agriculture===

[[Community-supported agriculture]] (CSA) has become increasingly important in Balisca, particularly for fruit and vegetable production. In the CSA model, consumers subscribe to receive regular shares of a farm's production, sharing both the risks and benefits of agricultural production. As of 2023, approximately 4.8 million households participated in CSA programs.

===Urban agriculture===

{{Main|Urban agriculture in Balisca}}

Urban agriculture expanded dramatically during the [[Special Period]] and has remained an important component of Balisca's food system. Urban agriculture takes multiple forms including community gardens on neighborhood-based communal land, rooftop farms with intensive production on building rooftops, vertical farms using indoor production with hydroponic and aeroponic systems, and peri-urban cooperatives with larger-scale production on urban fringes.

Urban agriculture produces approximately 15% of Balisca's fresh vegetables and provides important social and educational benefits.

==Employment and rural society==

===Agricultural employment===

Employment in agriculture, forestry, and fishing has declined significantly since the [[Baliscan Revolution]], reflecting mechanization, productivity improvements, and structural economic change:

