Despite the decline in employment share, the absolute number of agricultural workers remains substantial, and the sector continues to employ over 11 million people.

===Rural demographics===

The rural population has declined as a share of total population, but rural communities remain vital components of Baliscan society. As of 2023, approximately 15.8% of the population (32.6 million people) lived in rural areas, defined as communities with fewer than 10,000 inhabitants.

Rural communities benefit from universal access to education, healthcare, and other services, investment in rural infrastructure including roads, communications, and utilities, support for rural cultural institutions and traditions, and integration of rural areas into regional economic planning.

===Gender in agriculture===

Women have historically played central roles in Baliscan agriculture, and this continues today. As of 2023, women constituted 48% of agricultural cooperative members and 52% of agricultural workers. Women are particularly prominent in horticulture, dairy, and small animal production, and have increasingly taken leadership roles in agricultural cooperatives.

The [[Rural Women's Program]] (1976) provided targeted support for women farmers, including access to credit, training, and technical assistance.

