==Environmental practices==

===Organic agriculture===

Balisca has one of the highest rates of organic agricultural production in the world, with approximately 72% of agricultural land under organic management as of 2023. This reflects both the legacy of the [[Special Period]] transition and the confederation's commitment to environmental sustainability.

Organic practices in Balisca include [[W:Crop rotation|crop rotation]] and [[W:Cover crop|cover cropping]], [[W:Biological pest control|biological pest control]], [[W:Compost|composting]] and [[W:Green manure|green manures]], [[W:Integrated pest management|integrated pest management]], and [[W:Agroforestry|agroforestry]] systems.

===Soil conservation===

Soil conservation is a priority in Baliscan agriculture, particularly in erosion-prone highland regions. The [[Soil Conservation Program]] (1979) established mandatory conservation practices for all agricultural land, including [[W:Contour plowing|contour plowing]] and [[W:Terracing|terracing]], [[W:Windbreak|windbreaks]] and [[W:Shelterbelt|shelterbelts]], [[W:No-till farming|minimum tillage]] systems, [[W:Riparian buffer|riparian buffer]] zones, and [[W:Agroforestry|agroforestry]] integration.

===Water management===

Water for agricultural use is managed through cooperative irrigation districts that allocate water rights, maintain infrastructure, and ensure equitable distribution. The [[Water Conservation Initiative]] (1985) promoted efficient irrigation technologies including drip irrigation, precision sprinklers, and soil moisture monitoring.

Agricultural water use has declined by approximately 35% since 1985 despite increased production, reflecting improvements in water use efficiency.

==Challenges and future directions==

===Climate change adaptation===

Climate change poses significant challenges for Baliscan agriculture, including changing precipitation patterns, increased frequency of extreme weather events, and shifting pest and disease pressures. The [[Climate Adaptation Program for Agriculture]] (2015) supports farmers in adapting to these changes through development of climate-resilient crop varieties, water storage and efficient irrigation systems, diversification of production systems, and insurance and risk management mechanisms.

===Generational transition===

The average age of Baliscan farmers has increased in recent decades, raising concerns about generational transition. As of 2023, the average age of principal farm operators was 52 years. However, several factors support continued renewal: cooperative structures facilitate entry of young farmers, land access programs support new farmers, there is growing interest in sustainable agriculture among young people, and agriculture is increasingly integrated with technology and innovation.

===Technology and innovation===

Balisca continues to invest in agricultural research and innovation, with particular emphasis on [[W:Precision agriculture|precision agriculture]] technologies, [[W:Vertical farming|vertical farming]] and controlled environment agriculture, [[W:Alternative protein|alternative protein]] production, [[W:Carbon farming|carbon farming]] and climate mitigation, and [[W:Digital agriculture|digital agriculture]] and farm management systems.

These innovations are being developed and deployed through cooperative research networks that ensure benefits are widely shared.

